course: dictionary
lesson: 6201
title: Misremember and remember wrongly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To misremember something is to remember it wrongly. Your memory mixes up the facts, so the picture in your head is not quite what really happened. Usually this is an honest mistake, and nobody does it on purpose.
explain: Look at the start of the word: mis- means wrongly, as in misspell, misread and misunderstand. So misremember is remember with mis- added. You might misremember a name, a number or the day of a party.
explain: «A person who "misremembered" may simply have said something false.» Here the word describes a memory that has gone wrong, but sometimes people use it to cover a lie. The context clues help you decide: does the sentence sound like an accident, or like a choice?
fact: Misremember means to remember something wrongly.
fact: Mis- is a prefix that means wrongly, as in misspell, misread and misunderstand.
fact: Misremember is a verb: misremember, misremembers, misremembered, misremembering.
fact: «Misremember» is a US official way to say someone remembered wrongly or lied about the past.
fact: In a maths problem a character may misremember a number, so you must spot the wrong one.
exercise: Ella is sure her gran's birthday is in May, but it is really in June. «Ella has misremembered the date.» What does misremember mean here? | To tell someone the date on purpose | *To remember something wrongly | To write the date in a book | To forget the date completely
exercise: What does the prefix mis- mean in misremember? | Very much | Again | *Wrongly | Under
exercise: «A politician says he "misremembered" the meeting.» Why should a careful reader check the context? | Because it means he forgot his own name | Because the word always means he was very clever | *Because the word can sometimes hide a lie | Because the word is a happy one
test: Which meaning fits misremember best? | To write something down carefully | *To remember something wrongly | To tell someone a secret | To forget a memory completely
test: In «A person who "misremembered" may simply have said something false», what might misremembered suggest? | A plan for the future | A cheerful memory of a party | *A memory that went wrong, or a lie covered up | A word in a dictionary
exam: Mia says the bus is number 27, but the sign says 72. What is the best word for her mistake? | Misplace | Mispronounce | *Misremember | Misspell
